[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRRouting (BGP control plane) on router1. The FRR daemon responsible for establishing the eBGP session with router2 is down.

Mechanism: With BGP down on router1, no inter-AS routes are exchanged. Router1’s routing table contains only its connected networks (193.10.11.0/24 and 195.11.14.0/24), and router2 only knows 193.10.11.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24. Thus, traffic from pc1 (195.11.14.2) to pc2 (200.1.1.2) is forwarded to router1 but is dropped because router1 lacks a route to 200.1.1.0/24. Conversely, router2 has no route back to 195.11.14.0/24. This matches the user’s observation that pc1 can still ping its gateway (L2/L3 local) while the partner side remains healthy locally (pc2 to 200.1.1.1).

Localization: router1 control plane (FRR service). Evidence: systemctl shows frr inactive (dead) on router1; vtysh indicates “bgpd is not running” and no BGP config present. The inter-router link is up (router1 can ping router2 on 193.10.11.2). On router2, FRR is running and BGP shows neighbor 193.10.11.1 in Active (attempting, but remote is not responding).

Root cause: The FRR service on router1 is stopped, leaving no active BGP process to peer with router2.

Corrective fix: Start and enable FRR on router1 (e.g., systemctl start frr; systemctl enable frr) and ensure BGP is configured (AS 1, neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2, advertise 195.11.14.0/24). Verify the session reaches Established and routes to 200.1.1.0/24 appear on router1 (and 195.11.14.0/24 on router2). 
=== END ===